    ($SATQ$81f09e07$SATQ$, $SATQ$d679edb0-8a6b-44bb-9ae3-78407013c7e9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Instead of sleeping on soft pillows, ancient Egyptians slept on hard, elevated headrests, their design <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> airflow, protection from insects, and hairstyle maintenance over comfort.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9c4ebaa0-be48-4ca6-8d7e-dd72ba5d8864","content":"<p>had prioritized</p>"},{"id":"acfff34c-1010-4105-b22e-b80eb4373ed3","content":"<p>was prioritizing</p>"},{"id":"fcc5b6c4-4bbb-41d7-9fe7-6ec381ab05f2","content":"<p>prioritized</p>"},{"id":"f10233f0-93e4-4624-9d0b-9edca5125428","content":"<p>prioritizing</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f10233f0-93e4-4624-9d0b-9edca5125428"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite present participle &ldquo;prioritizing&rdquo; is correctly used to form a supplementary element that modifies &ldquo;their design,&rdquo; describing what the headrests&rsquo; design prioritized.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. The finite past perfect verb &ldquo;had prioritized&rdquo; creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the comma after &ldquo;headrests&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. The finite past progressive verb &ldquo;was prioritizing&rdquo; creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the comma after &ldquo;headrests&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. The finite past tense verb &ldquo;prioritized&rdquo; creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the comma after &ldquo;headrests&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"81f09e07","external_id":"d679edb0-8a6b-44bb-9ae3-78407013c7e9","vaultid":null,"uId":"f1bb4e65-aa0c-4a6c-a69a-92b7704185cd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Instead of sleeping on soft pillows, ancient Egyptians slept on hard, elevated headrests, their design <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> airflow, protection from insects, and hairstyle maintenance over comfort.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9c4ebaa0-be48-4ca6-8d7e-dd72ba5d8864","content":"<p>had prioritized</p>"},{"id":"acfff34c-1010-4105-b22e-b80eb4373ed3","content":"<p>was prioritizing</p>"},{"id":"fcc5b6c4-4bbb-41d7-9fe7-6ec381ab05f2","content":"<p>prioritized</p>"},{"id":"f10233f0-93e4-4624-9d0b-9edca5125428","content":"<p>prioritizing</p>"}],"keys":["f10233f0-93e4-4624-9d0b-9edca5125428"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite present participle &ldquo;prioritizing&rdquo; is correctly used to form a supplementary element that modifies &ldquo;their design,&rdquo; describing what the headrests&rsquo; design prioritized.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. The finite past perfect verb &ldquo;had prioritized&rdquo; creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the comma after &ldquo;headrests&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. The finite past progressive verb &ldquo;was prioritizing&rdquo; creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the comma after &ldquo;headrests&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. The finite past tense verb &ldquo;prioritized&rdquo; creates a second main clause in the sentence, and the comma after &ldquo;headrests&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$82a76537$SATQ$, $SATQ$9769fda4-5abb-4b85-9e45-26e6b0d6c2a2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Key-value pairs are an important aspect of JavaScript Object Notation (JSON), an electronic file format for storing and transmitting data. Keys function as labels, while values contain the actual information. In a JSON file storing data about fire belly <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> instance, you could encounter a key such as &ldquo;species&rdquo; with the associated value of &ldquo;<em>Cynops orientalis</em>.&rdquo;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"45947133-fc97-43b0-8fff-f17e081d85ae","content":"<p>newts<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span> For&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"437cceb6-7311-4c7c-8a7c-f8ba40726f03","content":"<p>newts<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> for</p>"},{"id":"8df244a8-c25b-417c-b08a-1374f997e5df","content":"<p>newts<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span> for</p>"},{"id":"5467d17b-46f1-4b9b-ac29-04df5c6fc684","content":"<p>newts<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> for</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["437cceb6-7311-4c7c-8a7c-f8ba40726f03"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. The comma after &ldquo;newts&rdquo; pairs with the comma after &ldquo;instance&rdquo; to separate the supplementary element &ldquo;for instance&rdquo; from the rest of the sentence. The supplementary element appears between the prepositional phrase (&ldquo;In&hellip;newts&rdquo;) and the main clause of the sentence (&ldquo;you&hellip;<em>orientalis</em>&rdquo;) and signals that the sentence is presenting an example of how keys and values function in JSON files.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a sentence fragment (&ldquo;In a JSON file storing data about fire belly newts&rdquo;). Choice C is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element &ldquo;for instance&rdquo; from the rest of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element &ldquo;for instance&rdquo; from the rest of the sentence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"82a76537","external_id":"9769fda4-5abb-4b85-9e45-26e6b0d6c2a2","vaultid":null,"uId":"273bf8dd-5c46-421e-9920-3b29f483f2f7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Key-value pairs are an important aspect of JavaScript Object Notation (JSON), an electronic file format for storing and transmitting data. Keys function as labels, while values contain the actual information. In a JSON file storing data about fire belly <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> instance, you could encounter a key such as &ldquo;species&rdquo; with the associated value of &ldquo;<em>Cynops orientalis</em>.&rdquo;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"45947133-fc97-43b0-8fff-f17e081d85ae","content":"<p>newts<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span> For&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"437cceb6-7311-4c7c-8a7c-f8ba40726f03","content":"<p>newts<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> for</p>"},{"id":"8df244a8-c25b-417c-b08a-1374f997e5df","content":"<p>newts<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span> for</p>"},{"id":"5467d17b-46f1-4b9b-ac29-04df5c6fc684","content":"<p>newts<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> for</p>"}],"keys":["437cceb6-7311-4c7c-8a7c-f8ba40726f03"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. The comma after &ldquo;newts&rdquo; pairs with the comma after &ldquo;instance&rdquo; to separate the supplementary element &ldquo;for instance&rdquo; from the rest of the sentence. The supplementary element appears between the prepositional phrase (&ldquo;In&hellip;newts&rdquo;) and the main clause of the sentence (&ldquo;you&hellip;<em>orientalis</em>&rdquo;) and signals that the sentence is presenting an example of how keys and values function in JSON files.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a sentence fragment (&ldquo;In a JSON file storing data about fire belly newts&rdquo;). Choice C is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element &ldquo;for instance&rdquo; from the rest of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element &ldquo;for instance&rdquo; from the rest of the sentence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$831b55ec$SATQ$, $SATQ$6210f31b-209c-4eef-8eb5-f4adb93c6abb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Louise Bennett (1919&ndash;2006), also known as &ldquo;Miss Lou,&rdquo; was an influential Jamaican poet and folklorist. Her innovative poems <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the use of Jamaican Creole (a spoken language) in literature.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c2164deb-0262-4738-8028-b348174de44b","content":"<p>popularized;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"19849e6e-8185-46ed-8956-0cd5ae59a22d","content":"<p>popularized,</p>"},{"id":"7601650b-68d8-4fdf-af9e-436ff2c9c8ba","content":"<p>popularized</p>"},{"id":"54b73edc-bd36-4495-a4aa-7110e519bfce","content":"<p>popularized:</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7601650b-68d8-4fdf-af9e-436ff2c9c8ba"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a verb and an object. No punctuation is needed between the verb "popularized" and its object "the use of Jamaican Creole." The object helps complete the idea of the verb&mdash;in this case, it explains what Louise Bennett popularized&mdash;and any punctuation between the two results in an ungrammatical sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"831b55ec","external_id":"6210f31b-209c-4eef-8eb5-f4adb93c6abb","vaultid":null,"uId":"cbeb9fc9-be4f-47cb-ab50-9619ed25aefb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Louise Bennett (1919&ndash;2006), also known as &ldquo;Miss Lou,&rdquo; was an influential Jamaican poet and folklorist. Her innovative poems <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the use of Jamaican Creole (a spoken language) in literature.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c2164deb-0262-4738-8028-b348174de44b","content":"<p>popularized;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"19849e6e-8185-46ed-8956-0cd5ae59a22d","content":"<p>popularized,</p>"},{"id":"7601650b-68d8-4fdf-af9e-436ff2c9c8ba","content":"<p>popularized</p>"},{"id":"54b73edc-bd36-4495-a4aa-7110e519bfce","content":"<p>popularized:</p>"}],"keys":["7601650b-68d8-4fdf-af9e-436ff2c9c8ba"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a verb and an object. No punctuation is needed between the verb \"popularized\" and its object \"the use of Jamaican Creole.\" The object helps complete the idea of the verb&mdash;in this case, it explains what Louise Bennett popularized&mdash;and any punctuation between the two results in an ungrammatical sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323049,"updateDate":1730147323049}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323049, 1730147323049),
    ($SATQ$83617355$SATQ$, $SATQ$eebf8e2e-dd88-4f72-911b-ab8ed88ed646$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Each night in Gij&oacute;n, Spain, a section of the city&rsquo;s marina is bathed in a soft green glow. The source of the glow is the &Aacute;rbol de la Sidra, a large sculpture made up of 3,200 recycled glass bottles. A lamp inside the tree-shaped structure <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the green glass.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6182342d-833f-4b1f-ac8e-4d945ef6f8c2","content":"<p>will be illuminating</p>"},{"id":"67b60ba9-5b89-42df-a046-86f8faac09be","content":"<p>illuminates</p>"},{"id":"8d9381aa-d8f0-437d-82e3-e8ac2562d188","content":"<p>would illuminate</p>"},{"id":"b3f72a57-1d5f-43e3-be5a-1afeb4b48228","content":"<p>illuminated</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["67b60ba9-5b89-42df-a046-86f8faac09be"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verbs to express tense. In this choice, the present tense verb "illuminates" is consistent with the other present tense verb ("is") used to describe the sculpture, correctly indicating that the sculpture habitually glows ("each night") and that the lamp inside is the source of its illumination.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the future progressive tense verb "will be illuminating" isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the sculpture and the source of its glow. Choice C is incorrect because the modal  "would," which is used to indicate a typical behavior in the past, isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the sculpture and the source of its glow. Choice D is incorrect because the past tense verb "illuminated" isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the sculpture and the source of its glow.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"83617355","external_id":"eebf8e2e-dd88-4f72-911b-ab8ed88ed646","vaultid":null,"uId":"1633930e-86f1-47a1-832e-aa929accdc42","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Each night in Gij&oacute;n, Spain, a section of the city&rsquo;s marina is bathed in a soft green glow. The source of the glow is the &Aacute;rbol de la Sidra, a large sculpture made up of 3,200 recycled glass bottles. A lamp inside the tree-shaped structure <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the green glass.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6182342d-833f-4b1f-ac8e-4d945ef6f8c2","content":"<p>will be illuminating</p>"},{"id":"67b60ba9-5b89-42df-a046-86f8faac09be","content":"<p>illuminates</p>"},{"id":"8d9381aa-d8f0-437d-82e3-e8ac2562d188","content":"<p>would illuminate</p>"},{"id":"b3f72a57-1d5f-43e3-be5a-1afeb4b48228","content":"<p>illuminated</p>"}],"keys":["67b60ba9-5b89-42df-a046-86f8faac09be"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verbs to express tense. In this choice, the present tense verb \"illuminates\" is consistent with the other present tense verb (\"is\") used to describe the sculpture, correctly indicating that the sculpture habitually glows (\"each night\") and that the lamp inside is the source of its illumination.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the future progressive tense verb \"will be illuminating\" isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the sculpture and the source of its glow. Choice C is incorrect because the modal  \"would,\" which is used to indicate a typical behavior in the past, isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the sculpture and the source of its glow. Choice D is incorrect because the past tense verb \"illuminated\" isn&rsquo;t consistent with the other present tense verb used to describe the sculpture and the source of its glow.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323049,"updateDate":1730147323049}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323049, 1730147323049),
    ($SATQ$83898524$SATQ$, $SATQ$79863ab0-1eb0-4a5f-ada0-744b9137e41b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$1189d8da-199d-44fb-baad-d650fa059748$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In addition to advocating for South America&rsquo;s independence in two political treatises, the <em>Cartagena Manifesto</em> and the <em>Letter from Jamaica,</em> Sim&oacute;n Bol&iacute;var personally led armies against the Spanish, liberating three South American territories&mdash;New Granada (present-day Colombia and Panama), Venezuela, and Quito (present-day <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> from colonial rule.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"db1d633b-2e55-4482-8698-25e98f342b7e","content":"<p>Ecuador,)</p>"},{"id":"8b69e4e1-8698-4020-9966-39131df2a000","content":"<p>Ecuador)</p>"},{"id":"aa70e66b-7b83-4cd6-a4ce-319e508060b3","content":"<p>Ecuador),</p>"},{"id":"e413eead-4a19-44f7-acf7-a5220757224f","content":"<p>Ecuador)&mdash;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e413eead-4a19-44f7-acf7-a5220757224f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of a supplementary element within a sentence. The dash after &ldquo;Ecuador&rdquo; and the closing parenthesis pairs with the dash after &ldquo;territories&rdquo; to separate the supplementary element (&ldquo;New&hellip;Ecuador&rdquo;) from the rest of the sentence. The supplementary element specifies the three South American territories that Sim&oacute;n Bol&iacute;var liberated, and the pair of dashes indicates that this element could be removed without affecting the grammatical coherence of the sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Furthermore, punctuation isn&rsquo;t needed between &ldquo;Ecuador&rdquo; and the closing parenthesis.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because a comma can&rsquo;t be paired with a dash to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-796-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$58dffa2e-6498-4caf-9302-a6f3be2e0b5f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"83898524","external_id":"79863ab0-1eb0-4a5f-ada0-744b9137e41b","vaultid":"1189d8da-199d-44fb-baad-d650fa059748","uId":"2d3a85cb-1b34-49ac-b3ba-dca42c805630","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In addition to advocating for South America&rsquo;s independence in two political treatises, the <em>Cartagena Manifesto</em> and the <em>Letter from Jamaica,</em> Sim&oacute;n Bol&iacute;var personally led armies against the Spanish, liberating three South American territories&mdash;New Granada (present-day Colombia and Panama), Venezuela, and Quito (present-day <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> from colonial rule.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"db1d633b-2e55-4482-8698-25e98f342b7e","content":"<p>Ecuador,)</p>"},{"id":"8b69e4e1-8698-4020-9966-39131df2a000","content":"<p>Ecuador)</p>"},{"id":"aa70e66b-7b83-4cd6-a4ce-319e508060b3","content":"<p>Ecuador),</p>"},{"id":"e413eead-4a19-44f7-acf7-a5220757224f","content":"<p>Ecuador)&mdash;</p>"}],"keys":["e413eead-4a19-44f7-acf7-a5220757224f"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of a supplementary element within a sentence. The dash after &ldquo;Ecuador&rdquo; and the closing parenthesis pairs with the dash after &ldquo;territories&rdquo; to separate the supplementary element (&ldquo;New&hellip;Ecuador&rdquo;) from the rest of the sentence. The supplementary element specifies the three South American territories that Sim&oacute;n Bol&iacute;var liberated, and the pair of dashes indicates that this element could be removed without affecting the grammatical coherence of the sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Furthermore, punctuation isn&rsquo;t needed between &ldquo;Ecuador&rdquo; and the closing parenthesis.&nbsp;Choice B is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because a comma can&rsquo;t be paired with a dash to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-796-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"58dffa2e-6498-4caf-9302-a6f3be2e0b5f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959353,"updateDate":1691007959353}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959353, 1691007959353),
    ($SATQ$83c9c503$SATQ$, $SATQ$d330987d-701c-4103-b3d5-10c01e98bd35$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Nigerian American artist Toyin Ojih Odutola uses black-ink pens to create highly detailed drawings of human figures. Her portrait of novelist Zadie <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> is displayed in the National Portrait Gallery in London.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b2a85a8f-ecee-4303-87f5-1ad74d1bbda9","content":"<p>Smith<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span></p>"},{"id":"2b85fbc1-f552-4bfe-8fc0-2147bbea85f3","content":"<p>Smith<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">&mdash;</span></p>"},{"id":"fed26d5f-3ac9-4df0-b333-d834e96c5589","content":"<p>Smith</p>"},{"id":"b0eb7fe8-dbfd-4dda-9cc1-45e4a657144e","content":"<p>Smith<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["fed26d5f-3ac9-4df0-b333-d834e96c5589"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a subject and a verb. No punctuation is needed when, as in this case, a subject ("Her portrait of novelist Zadie Smith") is immediately followed by a main verb ("is displayed").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"83c9c503","external_id":"d330987d-701c-4103-b3d5-10c01e98bd35","vaultid":null,"uId":"8a8b1fc2-3944-4210-b7bf-d41bf52c7e8a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Nigerian American artist Toyin Ojih Odutola uses black-ink pens to create highly detailed drawings of human figures. Her portrait of novelist Zadie <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> is displayed in the National Portrait Gallery in London.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b2a85a8f-ecee-4303-87f5-1ad74d1bbda9","content":"<p>Smith<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span></p>"},{"id":"2b85fbc1-f552-4bfe-8fc0-2147bbea85f3","content":"<p>Smith<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">&mdash;</span></p>"},{"id":"fed26d5f-3ac9-4df0-b333-d834e96c5589","content":"<p>Smith</p>"},{"id":"b0eb7fe8-dbfd-4dda-9cc1-45e4a657144e","content":"<p>Smith<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}],"keys":["fed26d5f-3ac9-4df0-b333-d834e96c5589"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a subject and a verb. No punctuation is needed when, as in this case, a subject (\"Her portrait of novelist Zadie Smith\") is immediately followed by a main verb (\"is displayed\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669213,"updateDate":1743430669213}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669213, 1743430669213),
    ($SATQ$843f92af$SATQ$, $SATQ$7e42cdd1-ac80-4b64-be06-13a9faac854d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4b1f7e8e-bb59-4252-b934-323cc1672e92$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The sun never sets during the Arctic summer in the Far North. In response, reindeer in this region must change their sleep habits. Instead of resting when it gets dark, they rest when they need <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> their food.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bd1a03c3-7a2c-428a-817c-c413c1e29a1f","content":"<p>digest</p>"},{"id":"1bc71909-4b8b-4881-a38a-0aced598a3ab","content":"<p>will digest</p>"},{"id":"eb91ae2f-6691-4605-a05f-aee246c1c9fb","content":"<p>to digest</p>"},{"id":"ac1c9bbc-d444-4fe8-923c-0ae01f12d8fc","content":"<p>digesting</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["eb91ae2f-6691-4605-a05f-aee246c1c9fb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of nonfinite verb forms within a sentence. Working together with the finite verb "need," the nonfinite to-infinitive verb "to digest" is correctly used to form a subordinate clause that describes what the reindeer need.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the verb "digest" (in either its finite or nonfinite form) can&rsquo;t be used in this way with the finite verb "need." Choice B is incorrect because the finite verb "will digest" can&rsquo;t be used in this way with the finite verb "need." Choice D is incorrect because the nonfinite participle "digesting" can&rsquo;t be used in this way with the finite verb "need."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-756-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b0e66b58-4bcd-4149-b251-3f801806354d$SATQ$::u