 verb \"need.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-756-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b0e66b58-4bcd-4149-b251-3f801806354d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959351,"updateDate":1691007959351}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959351, 1691007959351),
    ($SATQ$8459dc2f$SATQ$, $SATQ$e8cc79a3-b745-4fd7-ac84-1094a8931ca9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>When using a search engine, many people click just the first one or two results. Click restraint is the practice of scanning a search results page and evaluating what you see before deciding which link <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2cfe9717-9462-4db7-bb23-dd7413f7d606","content":"<p>should you choose<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"},{"id":"984ac676-a616-4f7d-a062-1e0b2c1a19c3","content":"<p>you should choose<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"53297def-eafd-43a2-835a-a08c66a20232","content":"<p>should you choose<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"c7366ff1-f184-44a6-86c3-51c0875514f0","content":"<p>you should choose<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c7366ff1-f184-44a6-86c3-51c0875514f0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is end-of-sentence punctuation. This choice correctly uses a period to punctuate a declarative sentence that ends with an indirect question (&ldquo;which link you should choose.&rdquo;)</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The structure requires a declarative clause that presents an indirect question about what is evaluated in click restraint, not an interrogative clause that presents a direct question, such as &ldquo;which link should you choose.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect. It&rsquo;s unconventional to use a question mark in this way to punctuate a declarative clause that presents an indirect question, such as &ldquo;which link you should choose.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect. The structure requires a declarative clause that presents an indirect question about what is evaluated in click restraint, not an interrogative clause that presents a direct question, such as &ldquo;which link should you choose.&rdquo;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"8459dc2f","external_id":"e8cc79a3-b745-4fd7-ac84-1094a8931ca9","vaultid":null,"uId":"b95e00e6-3fdd-4c17-989a-d60ff5450d62","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>When using a search engine, many people click just the first one or two results. Click restraint is the practice of scanning a search results page and evaluating what you see before deciding which link <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2cfe9717-9462-4db7-bb23-dd7413f7d606","content":"<p>should you choose<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"},{"id":"984ac676-a616-4f7d-a062-1e0b2c1a19c3","content":"<p>you should choose<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"53297def-eafd-43a2-835a-a08c66a20232","content":"<p>should you choose<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"c7366ff1-f184-44a6-86c3-51c0875514f0","content":"<p>you should choose<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"}],"keys":["c7366ff1-f184-44a6-86c3-51c0875514f0"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is end-of-sentence punctuation. This choice correctly uses a period to punctuate a declarative sentence that ends with an indirect question (&ldquo;which link you should choose.&rdquo;)</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The structure requires a declarative clause that presents an indirect question about what is evaluated in click restraint, not an interrogative clause that presents a direct question, such as &ldquo;which link should you choose.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect. It&rsquo;s unconventional to use a question mark in this way to punctuate a declarative clause that presents an indirect question, such as &ldquo;which link you should choose.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect. The structure requires a declarative clause that presents an indirect question about what is evaluated in click restraint, not an interrogative clause that presents a direct question, such as &ldquo;which link should you choose.&rdquo;</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$84658166$SATQ$, $SATQ$47afaaa9-8c03-407e-a86f-61898f5eb0be$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$651acf09-1d52-46c5-a6e7-435032ca7f71$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 1943, in the midst of World War II, mathematics professor Grace Hopper was recruited by the US military to help the war effort by solving complex equations. Hopper&rsquo;s subsequent career would involve more than just <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> as a pioneering computer programmer, Hopper would help usher in the digital age.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f73c3728-c659-43db-875e-e02cbec1af60","content":"<p>equations, though:</p>"},{"id":"0d2c8e5e-c5ca-428b-98eb-056764ba0dfe","content":"<p>equations, though,</p>"},{"id":"b51a1a28-c732-466e-96a9-05ea217bb44f","content":"<p>equations. Though,</p>"},{"id":"ef150ccb-863c-46d2-b430-24e20dc5eefd","content":"<p>equations though</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["f73c3728-c659-43db-875e-e02cbec1af60"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation to mark boundaries between supplements and clauses. The comma after &ldquo;equations&rdquo; is used to separate the independent clause (&ldquo;Hopper&rsquo;s&hellip;equation&rdquo;) from the supplementary adverb phrase &ldquo;though.&rdquo; The colon after &ldquo;though&rdquo; is used to mark the boundary between the clause ending with &ldquo;though&rdquo; and the following clause (&ldquo;as&hellip;age&rdquo;). A colon used in this way introduces information that illustrates or explains information that has come before it. In this case, the colon after &ldquo;though&rdquo; introduces the following explanation of how Hopper&rsquo;s subsequent career would involve more than just solving equations: she would become a pioneering computer programmer. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because&nbsp;it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two independent clauses (&ldquo;Hopper&rsquo;s&hellip;though&rdquo; and &ldquo;as&hellip;age&rdquo;) such as these. Choice C is incorrect because&nbsp;it results in an illogical sequence of sentences. Placing the period after &ldquo;equations&rdquo; and beginning the next sentence with &ldquo;Though&rdquo; illogically suggests that the following information (that Hopper would help usher in the digital age) is contrary to the information in the previous sentence (Hopper&rsquo;s subsequent career would involve more than just solving equations). Instead, the information that follows supports the information from the previous sentence by explaining how her work and influence extended beyond solely solving equations. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two independent clauses (&ldquo;Hopper&rsquo;s&hellip;though&rdquo; and &ldquo;as&hellip;age&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-682-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$5333f837-44c9-4e59-88dd-583b85461eb5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"84658166","external_id":"47afaaa9-8c03-407e-a86f-61898f5eb0be","vaultid":"651acf09-1d52-46c5-a6e7-435032ca7f71","uId":"9232c2d5-5f44-4aee-b290-666fa62fd1ce","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 1943, in the midst of World War II, mathematics professor Grace Hopper was recruited by the US military to help the war effort by solving complex equations. Hopper&rsquo;s subsequent career would involve more than just <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> as a pioneering computer programmer, Hopper would help usher in the digital age.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f73c3728-c659-43db-875e-e02cbec1af60","content":"<p>equations, though:</p>"},{"id":"0d2c8e5e-c5ca-428b-98eb-056764ba0dfe","content":"<p>equations, though,</p>"},{"id":"b51a1a28-c732-466e-96a9-05ea217bb44f","content":"<p>equations. Though,</p>"},{"id":"ef150ccb-863c-46d2-b430-24e20dc5eefd","content":"<p>equations though</p>"}],"keys":["f73c3728-c659-43db-875e-e02cbec1af60"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation to mark boundaries between supplements and clauses. The comma after &ldquo;equations&rdquo; is used to separate the independent clause (&ldquo;Hopper&rsquo;s&hellip;equation&rdquo;) from the supplementary adverb phrase &ldquo;though.&rdquo; The colon after &ldquo;though&rdquo; is used to mark the boundary between the clause ending with &ldquo;though&rdquo; and the following clause (&ldquo;as&hellip;age&rdquo;). A colon used in this way introduces information that illustrates or explains information that has come before it. In this case, the colon after &ldquo;though&rdquo; introduces the following explanation of how Hopper&rsquo;s subsequent career would involve more than just solving equations: she would become a pioneering computer programmer. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because&nbsp;it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two independent clauses (&ldquo;Hopper&rsquo;s&hellip;though&rdquo; and &ldquo;as&hellip;age&rdquo;) such as these. Choice C is incorrect because&nbsp;it results in an illogical sequence of sentences. Placing the period after &ldquo;equations&rdquo; and beginning the next sentence with &ldquo;Though&rdquo; illogically suggests that the following information (that Hopper would help usher in the digital age) is contrary to the information in the previous sentence (Hopper&rsquo;s subsequent career would involve more than just solving equations). Instead, the information that follows supports the information from the previous sentence by explaining how her work and influence extended beyond solely solving equations. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two independent clauses (&ldquo;Hopper&rsquo;s&hellip;though&rdquo; and &ldquo;as&hellip;age&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-682-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"5333f837-44c9-4e59-88dd-583b85461eb5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959346,"updateDate":1691007959346}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959346, 1691007959346),
    ($SATQ$856b495d$SATQ$, $SATQ$df6445af-a873-440f-a6e9-31de0e8f70f7$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$922add41-79c2-4d10-b391-abe274fefa3c$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the early twentieth century, Joseph Kekuku and other Hawaiian <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in the mainland United States to the bright and lilting sound of the <span lang="haw"><em>kīkā kila</em></span>, or Hawaiian steel guitar. The instrument soon became a fixture in American blues and country music.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c3485104-ba03-45b2-84ec-5e5396b427ab","content":"<p>musicians introduced audiences</p>"},{"id":"255c3936-a79d-42ae-9bdd-3b028188f843","content":"<p>musicians&rsquo; introduced audiences&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"03604f86-d0a7-425b-b718-ec60323f47f3","content":"<p>musician&rsquo;s introduced audience&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"e76966a8-4eff-4ddc-9d3c-6fc5b541d29a","content":"<p>musicians&rsquo; introduced audiences</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c3485104-ba03-45b2-84ec-5e5396b427ab"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of plural nouns. The plural nouns "musicians" and "audiences" correctly indicate that there were multiple musicians introducing the music to multiple audiences.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the plural nouns "musicians" and "audiences," not the plural possessive nouns "musicians&rsquo;" and "audiences&rsquo;." Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the plural nouns "musicians" and "audiences," not the singular possessive nouns "musician&rsquo;s" and "audience&rsquo;s." Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun "musicians," not the plural possessive noun "musicians&rsquo;."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-754-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b8c05286-9bcb-484b-99af-a3173adeb337$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"856b495d","external_id":"df6445af-a873-440f-a6e9-31de0e8f70f7","vaultid":"922add41-79c2-4d10-b391-abe274fefa3c","uId":"5d4572e3-7acc-4493-a249-3ecc6bb55f43","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the early twentieth century, Joseph Kekuku and other Hawaiian <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in the mainland United States to the bright and lilting sound of the <span lang=\"haw\"><em>kīkā kila</em></span>, or Hawaiian steel guitar. The instrument soon became a fixture in American blues and country music.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c3485104-ba03-45b2-84ec-5e5396b427ab","content":"<p>musicians introduced audiences</p>"},{"id":"255c3936-a79d-42ae-9bdd-3b028188f843","content":"<p>musicians&rsquo; introduced audiences&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"03604f86-d0a7-425b-b718-ec60323f47f3","content":"<p>musician&rsquo;s introduced audience&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"e76966a8-4eff-4ddc-9d3c-6fc5b541d29a","content":"<p>musicians&rsquo; introduced audiences</p>"}],"keys":["c3485104-ba03-45b2-84ec-5e5396b427ab"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of plural nouns. The plural nouns \"musicians\" and \"audiences\" correctly indicate that there were multiple musicians introducing the music to multiple audiences.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the plural nouns \"musicians\" and \"audiences,\" not the plural possessive nouns \"musicians&rsquo;\" and \"audiences&rsquo;.\" Choice C is incorrect because the context requires the plural nouns \"musicians\" and \"audiences,\" not the singular possessive nouns \"musician&rsquo;s\" and \"audience&rsquo;s.\" Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun \"musicians,\" not the plural possessive noun \"musicians&rsquo;.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-754-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b8c05286-9bcb-484b-99af-a3173adeb337","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959351,"updateDate":1691007959351}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959351, 1691007959351),
    ($SATQ$85b8b9c5$SATQ$, $SATQ$9b5de448-a962-4066-9d0a-de9dedcb59dd$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Many mechanical calculators were powered by a notched cylinder mechanism called the Leibniz wheel. Leibniz wheel calculators were popular in the first half of the twentieth <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> these ingenious devices were eventually replaced by electronic calculators.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9d83df1c-fe01-4a1b-9061-8f1f6fae31dd","content":"<p>century</p>"},{"id":"652799d2-2a14-4a79-bd4f-41d4205e8c07","content":"<p>century<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"6069554d-fe5c-4c52-82bc-e01fbebd8ec8","content":"<p>century<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> but</p>"},{"id":"2106033d-7469-4693-ac96-1c1019e3b7bb","content":"<p>century that</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6069554d-fe5c-4c52-82bc-e01fbebd8ec8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma and a coordinating conjunction ("but") to join the first main clause ("Leibniz...century") and the second main clause ("these ingenious...calculators").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice D is incorrect because joining the two main clauses in this way with the subordinating conjunction "that" results in an ungrammatical and illogical sentence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"85b8b9c5","external_id":"9b5de448-a962-4066-9d0a-de9dedcb59dd","vaultid":null,"uId":"697c5732-4d94-4b9d-8c31-9e3a091c599f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Many mechanical calculators were powered by a notched cylinder mechanism called the Leibniz wheel. Leibniz wheel calculators were popular in the first half of the twentieth <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> these ingenious devices were eventually replaced by electronic calculators.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9d83df1c-fe01-4a1b-9061-8f1f6fae31dd","content":"<p>century</p>"},{"id":"652799d2-2a14-4a79-bd4f-41d4205e8c07","content":"<p>century<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"6069554d-fe5c-4c52-82bc-e01fbebd8ec8","content":"<p>century<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> but</p>"},{"id":"2106033d-7469-4693-ac96-1c1019e3b7bb","content":"<p>century that</p>"}],"keys":["6069554d-fe5c-4c52-82bc-e01fbebd8ec8"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the coordination of main clauses within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma and a coordinating conjunction (\"but\") to join the first main clause (\"Leibniz...century\") and the second main clause (\"these ingenious...calculators\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The two main clauses are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Without a conjunction following it, a comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice D is incorrect because joining the two main clauses in this way with the subordinating conjunction \"that\" results in an ungrammatical and illogical sentence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669211,"updateDate":1743430669211}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669211, 1743430669211),
    ($SATQ$863065c7$SATQ$, $SATQ$1ed37cd6-0c97-4845-a120-92ca57b36b16$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Legal scholars James Melton and Tom Ginsburg&rsquo;s analysis of de jure judicial independence and its growth over decades <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> six constitutional features that enhance such independence, including judicial tenure and selection procedure. Albania&rsquo;s constitution contains five of these features.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1df85be3-1ddd-4dc3-91dd-ebd87ce61203","content":"<p>are identifying</p>"},{"id":"784a00a9-41d9-4f07-ac1c-5561bf0f2e23","content":"<p>identify</p>"},{"id":"dd8bad18-1518-4671-975f-2c750649a7eb","content":"<p>have identified</p>"},{"id":"67e0e5cf-ca9c-4c46-8773-a134265b4b96","content":"<p>identifies</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["67e0e5cf-ca9c-4c46-8773-a134265b4b96"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb "identifies" agrees in number with the singular subject "analysis."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb "are identifying" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "analysis." Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb "identify" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "analysis." Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb "have identified" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "analysis."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"863065c7","external_id":"1ed37cd6-0c97-4845-a120-92ca57b36b16","vaultid":null,"uId":"60aa4fda-1426-47f9-8ba1-7428b5f6f0b4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Legal scholars James Melton and Tom Ginsburg&rsquo;s analysis of de jure judicial independence and its growth over decades <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> six constitutional features that enhance such independence, including judicial tenure and selection procedure. Albania&rsquo;s constitution contains five of these features.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1df85be3-1ddd-4dc3-91dd-ebd87ce61203","content":"<p>are identifying</p>"},{"id":"784a00a9-41d9-4f07-ac1c-5561bf0f2e23","content":"<p>identify</p>"},{"id":"dd8bad18-1518-4671-975f-2c750649a7eb","content":"<p>have identified</p>"},{"id":"67e0e5cf-ca9c-4c46-8773-a134265b4b96","content":"<p>identifies</p>"}],"keys":["67e0e5cf-ca9c-4c46-8773-a134265b4b96"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb \"identifies\" agrees in number with the singular subject \"analysis.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb \"are identifying\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"analysis.\" Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb \"identify\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"analysis.\" Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb \"have identified\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"analysis.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061176,"updateDate":1755117061176}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061176, 1755117061176),
    ($SATQ$870ae7ec$SATQ$, $SATQ$7fbbb4df-76bf-4c0d-86da-6076a653b90d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f1e3a5d2-d605-443c-858b-a91ae56d1220$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Detroit natives Timothy Paule and Nicole Lindsey have combined their two passions, Detroit and beekeeping, to improve the health of their city&rsquo;s flowers and other vegetation. In 2017, the couple converted a vacant lot in the city into an <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in the years that followed they acquired nine additional lots and established more than 35 hives.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"661a8948-c1a5-4ed4-95bc-0941447d2b4e","content":"<p>apiary,</p>"},{"id":"45ed2c66-ca19-46a1-a950-22b834d30baa","content":"<p>apiary, and</p>"},{"id":"7f2038b5-007d-480e-a2df-2f8389f73bff","content":"<p>apiary and</p>"},{"id":"60509063-e6cb-4a5d-91f0-1a9e3345c068","content":"<p>apiary&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["45ed2c66-ca19-46a1-a950-22b834d30baa"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. Both clauses in this sentence could stand alone as complete sentences, which means they are both independent clauses. This choice uses a comma plus a coordinating conjunction to link them together, which is one of the correct ways to link two independent clauses.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice results in a run-on sentence error. Both clauses in this sentence could stand alone as complete sentences, which means they are both independent clauses. A comma by itself is not enough punctuation to link two independent clauses.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. This choice results in a run-on sentence error. Both clauses in this sentence could stand alone as complete sentences, which means they are both independent clauses. Independent clauses can only be linked in a few ways, including with a comma plus a coordinating conjunction. This choice uses the coordinating conjunction &ldquo;and,&rdquo; but it is missing the comma beforehand.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a run-on sentence error. Both clauses in this sentence could stand alone as complete sentences, which means they are both independent clauses. Independent clauses need to have certain kinds of punctuation marks between them. This choice doesn&rsquo;t use any punctuation between the two clauses.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-608-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$0b49e321-0b42-407d-be1d-94e43c052a88$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"870ae7ec","external_id":"7fbbb4df-76bf-4c0d-86da-6076a653b90d","vaultid":"f1e3a5d2-d605-443c-858b-a91ae56d1220","uId":"5de74a12-f448-447a-b328-4f9b2117a22c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Detroit natives Timothy Paule and Nicole Lindsey have combined their two passions, Detroit and beekeeping, to improve the health of their city&rsquo;s flowers and other vegetation. In 2017, the couple converted a vacant lot in the city into an <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in the years that followed they acquired nine additional lots and established more than 35 hives.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"661a8948-c1a5-4ed4-95bc-0941447d2b4e","content":"<p>apiary,</p>"},{"id":"45ed2c66-ca19-46a1-a950-22b834d30baa","content":"<p>apiary, and</p>"},{"id":"7f2038b5-007d-480e-a2df-2f8389f73bff","content":"<p>apiary and</p>"},{"id":"60509063-e6cb-4a5d-91f0-1a9e3345c068","content":"<p>apiary&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["45ed2c66-ca19-46a1-a950-22b834d30baa"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. Both clauses in this sentence could stand alone as complete sentences, which means they are both independent clauses. This choice uses a comma plus a coordinating conjunction to link them together, which is one of the correct ways to link two independent clauses.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice results in a run-on sentence error. Both clauses in this sentence could stand alone as complete sentences, which means they are both independent clauses. A comma by itself is not enough punctuation to link two independent clauses.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. This choice results in a run-on sentence error. Both clauses in this sentence could stand alone as complete sentences, which means they are both independent clauses. Independent clauses can only be linked in a few ways, including with a comma plus a coordinating conjunction. This choice uses the coordinating conjunction &ldquo;and,&rdquo; but it is missing the comma beforehand.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a run-on sentence error. Both clauses in this sentence could stand alone as complete sentences, which means they are both independent clauses. Independent clauses need to have certain kinds of punctuation marks between them. This choice doesn&rsquo;t use any punctuation between the two clauses.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-608-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"0b49e321-0b42-407d-be1d-94e43c052a88","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959339,"updateDate":1691007959339}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959339, 1691007959339)
